00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  German soldiers turnout for morning roll  call and sing the Marseillaise that's  nothing unusual at the Robert Schumann  barracks near the French border  corporal Marais has been serving in the  franco-german Brigade for four years now  he and the other officers know how  important it is for nations to work  together for these soldiers Europe is  part of their daily lives every day they  really feel they are Europeans singing  both national anthems is only one of the  features unique to this brigade every  day brings special challenges they have  the language barrier and always will we  have German soldiers who don't speak  French and vice-versa they have to build  bridges and they always have to remember  that something might not have been  understood during weapons training for  example one instructor explains and the  other translates marais originally comes  from Berlin he's just signed up for  another four years with the brigade he  has no trouble with the rifle drills we  have to learn to handle the French arms  and that means practice and the French  instruct us because they're French arms  the military routines of the two  countries have no differences as the  instructor explains this is a good  example this kind of thing happens here  every day in training we show each other  our equipment so everyone has a chance  to try it out the commanding officers  get ready to visit the troops they're  busy with a transport exercise the  officers communicate in English the  official language of NATO  everyone here speaks it we have no  special reports concerning incidents and  so on they are currently on their  mission English was also the working  language for the six months the  battalion was stationed in Lithuania for  NATO that was the high point of marae's  service to date he keeps some photos of  Lithuania on his cell phone on the road  to Strasbourg and France  another of the brigades bases supplies  where the troops are shuttled back and  forth across the River Rhine German and  French military traditions do differ  however the Germans are very progressive  a 21st century management style but the  French are still very military in  everything they do the forest camp is  closely guarded the commanding officer  turns up to inspect the exercise as a  German officer he serves in a military  force whose every mission has to be  approved by the Bundestag but his French  counterparts can be deployed on direct  orders from the French president this  difference can have political  ramifications  I know who pame is for me I see a  European military as a glimpse of the  future I think for many things it's  possible and necessary to work them out  in a political process if you ask us as  the military will say sure we can do it  overnight after all we've been doing it  for 30 years  the brigade has started traditions of  its own built a measure of confidence  created their own uniforms and songs  with verses in two languages its members  share a common spirit perhaps setting an  example for European military forces of  the future

